<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Pachana (Kaabu)</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the three royal provinces of the Kaabu Empire in present-day Guinea-Bissau, which took turns supplying the Mansaba of Kaabu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pachana was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Mandinka Kaabu Empire</a>, which grew from a westward extension of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and became independent in the 16th century. Like other Kaabu provinces, it is known mainly through oral tradition and occasional Portuguese reports.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pachana, Sama and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jimara-kaabu">Jimara</a> were the royal provinces whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyantio">Nyancho</a> warrior nobility, reckoned through the female line, provided the supreme king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a> in rotation. Its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> governed with lineage heads, and the Nyancho lived largely from war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> and trade with the Portuguese.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pachana declined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> pressure in the 19th century. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansala">Kansala</a> to the forces of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-jallon">Imamate of Futa Jallon</a> in 1867, its lands came under Fula rule and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:7.30%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mandinka traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandinka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Guinea-Bissau</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pachana, Pacana, Patiana</dd></dl></aside></div>

